db/id #edb/ref 17592186386662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b8ecd35-68f3-5c13-8e49-a99c29ed5cc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-113336" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\frac{6}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=-\\frac{6}{x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/d5/d5d93e12275c683e0a56f0ec9a0c99540a7774879cd0f8fa69151ddba68a44fa.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $6$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{6}{x}.$\n\n![](images/c5/c525edcf937bfdc311acd0d7cac778802e1939dc2bd61fd3a5bf58f808712220.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4640f978-9caa-5960-bec4-f153a1b4cc5d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92c55780-5247-5ab6-a621-d40a0527133d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ed/edb61b0239e926ae983d0cb67ffb05c7ff0b452564e6972dfa15cf0f2472a727.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386670} {:answer/id #uuid "96e6fd33-b036-50c8-a86d-2ed4364a0dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/65/65e24bd4cadacd7510d9dc529d13d02a40445abba54129a7f5801c75dd0e2da9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386671} {:answer/id #uuid "46d1074a-2a76-523e-ac49-c7030b1d984d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6c/6c4c728e59e416a589b85318988a3a87e2a3c9445b669c4091301b21254c87fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386672} {:answer/id #uuid "50a3028f-ed6c-5718-a36a-947dbee62ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/4612933db2d25ce80b7e7258d3e381fd03f951246b515c3c34c69b71c537c7fc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386673} {:answer/id #uuid "71df20dc-d7fc-5b41-be17-a6983196a882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d14d6a75f24afea6dfe31aed9dc752b470c6d672b6a3c0f97820738b6140e629.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92c55780-5247-5ab6-a621-d40a0527133d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ed/edb61b0239e926ae983d0cb67ffb05c7ff0b452564e6972dfa15cf0f2472a727.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386670} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6b93c17-1338-56db-a802-9f519be35fc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d34b2496-a69c-5cea-b687-ebc80f8f584a" :question/math-academy-id "q-113369" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{x}+2?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=-\\frac{5}{x}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/9d/9da0db2e6c97e507e82b7ca801e46802fabf91ad31ff53ad3cc9ae580151370a.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{x}.$\n\n![](images/46/4612933db2d25ce80b7e7258d3e381fd03f951246b515c3c34c69b71c537c7fc.png)\n\n- Translate it by $2$ unit up to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{x}+2.$\n\n![](images/ed/edb61b0239e926ae983d0cb67ffb05c7ff0b452564e6972dfa15cf0f2472a727.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4640f978-9caa-5960-bec4-f153a1b4cc5d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fc22b6a-72c5-57b6-baee-4019cb290f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/47/47a63204ef63a80905e5e6d2df5635a02d06694153cb67bc15eededf63c87b90.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386684} {:answer/id #uuid "765955f5-0542-50bc-9667-86b09f0b4e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3a8f77e96f836eadfdb1a0c73f35bad5e245b66df0f21daec6ac008fb69a4815.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386685} {:answer/id #uuid "f85feca0-357a-5c74-8fde-4d7fdaa2cd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f5/f558777e9ec384bdd169cddefeac589861cae2568f130816c4bf7a1f4ee1e520.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386686} {:answer/id #uuid "dba9cafa-0a6c-53c7-80a2-61fb5e35090a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8f/8f2a42430ac31a559e5f85c961579aa482280bd9b69ed5d948250f5fafce21a5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386687} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a9dbae-7498-55b8-b00b-07a41ab70457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0b/0bb082b243d961b45b63d352f84359d7022492df93e781f93114206e9924b5c2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f85feca0-357a-5c74-8fde-4d7fdaa2cd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f5/f558777e9ec384bdd169cddefeac589861cae2568f130816c4bf7a1f4ee1e520.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386686} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a9d5ff1-d590-522a-bcb5-31d743a32102" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "110d0b81-fd68-5dac-ba01-18cdf7c4cb65" :question/math-academy-id "q-113463" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{4(x-1)}?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=\\frac{3}{4(x-1)},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/e8/e80865b0f32a5473da27b39537034bcdabee0a6c24032f41413e20518642d3f4.png)\n\n- Translate it by $1$ unit to the right to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x-1}.$\n\n![](images/8f/8f2a42430ac31a559e5f85c961579aa482280bd9b69ed5d948250f5fafce21a5.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{3}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{4(x-1)}.$\n\n![](images/f5/f558777e9ec384bdd169cddefeac589861cae2568f130816c4bf7a1f4ee1e520.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "308f3685-0a90-56f3-9653-a83cec4286d2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Some Unknowns Given the Graph of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f0f5357-6a5d-52cb-a0b3-8bb0331ee994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244583} {:answer/id #uuid "606311eb-894b-5b77-b109-e32e121ff8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245155} {:answer/id #uuid "6d13ff09-b329-5200-85b0-89687a5270ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245277} {:answer/id #uuid "7f280c4b-6081-508f-8997-4749439fa6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245435} {:answer/id #uuid "847e53f3-ba86-5571-b86e-1f4f19fe8930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "606311eb-894b-5b77-b109-e32e121ff8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245155} :answer-field/id #uuid "917d77c5-3e45-5592-bf27-58d3f7e98794" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d881fc0-905f-5d7f-8a88-250eeb9291b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-113482" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d2dec8a05bc9835693c1df9dbc54618fd39d77667cbbdcd5bb6d92bf25c0d97.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y=\\frac{a}{x-b}+k.$ What is the value of $a+b+k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=0.5,$ while the vertical asymptote is $x=3.$ This means that $k=0.5$ and $b=3,$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x-3}+0.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(1,1.5)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x-3}+0.5 \\\\ 1.5 & =\\frac{a}{1-3}+0.5 \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{a}{-2} \\\\ a & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b+k=-2+3+0.5=1.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064203 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2b47545-fdf1-5e6a-b0bb-bbbecb8067cc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Positive Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "326be129-c932-54e7-bd6e-d61f8a6c1750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{5}(2-5x)^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398104} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f6915c-a30b-538c-be4c-c5d4feafc456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}(2-5x)^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398105} {:answer/id #uuid "d929af17-b6b6-5bb1-9948-38e8c5714648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}(2-5x)^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398106} {:answer/id #uuid "e257789f-7b07-5075-adc9-57e60483dc0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}(2-5x)^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398107} {:answer/id #uuid "65143245-b12a-5ed5-a1a3-dc55de2b261a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15}(2-5x)^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8f6915c-a30b-538c-be4c-c5d4feafc456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}(2-5x)^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398105} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d582062-813e-5b4d-b116-6b4cb9d5d390" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "201b1303-edfe-5a94-b70a-3687fd03d284" :question/math-academy-id "q-113502" :question/problem "$\\int 18(2-5x)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 18(2-5x)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 18{u}^{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})\\int {u}^{5}\\cdot \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}{u}^{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}(2-5x)^{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{3}{5}(2-5x)^{6}+C] & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{3}{5}(2-5x)^{6}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}(2-5x)^{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}(2-5x)^{5}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =18(2-5x)^{5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064204 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87bb5e34-8a7e-5077-9056-44171c31d68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398111} {:answer/id #uuid "73df82c1-76f6-50ea-99b9-53a33ba7607d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398112} {:answer/id #uuid "cfab3df7-4bb6-5eeb-9342-3841991827b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398113} {:answer/id #uuid "1e49762e-1e14-5248-8c9e-16a5e673bda1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398114} {:answer/id #uuid "15a56691-9975-5ae2-aa7b-fda010fe63b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15a56691-9975-5ae2-aa7b-fda010fe63b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398115} :answer-field/id #uuid "c13dea0f-f218-5cd2-a503-c7b2250d51fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1948aa2d-5fbb-59f5-8ccc-6dd9cfd6ef51" :question/math-academy-id "q-113509" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (x-2)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=x-2.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x-2)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(x-2)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (x-2)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-2)+0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064204 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f0d601-5b9e-5b34-b1e4-35332ff61132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20(9-5x)^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398118} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0f063f-eba9-55db-a617-104bbe47e493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5(9-5x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398119} {:answer/id #uuid "199a03cb-71ce-557c-b3af-135bd3564f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398120} {:answer/id #uuid "418bd3f4-e4c0-5bc7-b4ac-5541696af9d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398121} {:answer/id #uuid "51978e0d-c725-5111-8fd8-58bfe89fa4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2(9-5x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "418bd3f4-e4c0-5bc7-b4ac-5541696af9d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398121} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b49b60e-8217-5844-bea9-fe48c12aaf25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d34fadec-e1db-5f0e-b3fb-64f3326f1376" :question/math-academy-id "q-113517" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{(9-5x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (9-5x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=9-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (9-5x)^{-3}\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}){u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10{u}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(9-5x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot (-2)(9-5x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(9-5x)^{-3}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =(9-5x)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(9-5x)}^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a12e93a-5479-5bc8-a172-5f001b81680d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399797} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd56062-f5eb-5d51-97c9-2401cc2382c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399798} {:answer/id #uuid "50434bd2-8dc8-5df0-b8fa-4d7fa1f9bf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399799} {:answer/id #uuid "060d6765-1a4c-5cef-9036-baa44df95b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399800} {:answer/id #uuid "0532888d-2587-5d82-aa30-da64f92b3ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50434bd2-8dc8-5df0-b8fa-4d7fa1f9bf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399799} :answer-field/id #uuid "9de2cff7-92c8-5c70-9102-18ed3f31a5ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "046b8096-a4b3-5639-81fd-05cfaf3fcc14" :question/math-academy-id "q-113527" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+6x-12}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+6x-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-2)+6(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-3)}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+6)(x-2)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-2=0$ gives the solution $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+6=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064208 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af32f07f-fa65-5ede-a506-735dc2d3a5ef" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Combination of Linear Rational Functions Using the General Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bdfb622-49b8-594f-a384-42995cb2c4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(3x+2)(3x-2)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244424} {:answer/id #uuid "44aada26-4d55-57df-82e1-467968cd4443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(3x+2)(3x-2)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244917} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7c324f-ce33-58d1-aeaf-8e1275376ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(3x+2)(3x-2)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245116} {:answer/id #uuid "96e82d2d-4206-5a6a-b1a4-4d0423e61b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{3x+2}{3x-2}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245644} {:answer/id #uuid "cd50755b-b627-54de-8de5-5509a4cc9a9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{3x+2}{3x-2}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44aada26-4d55-57df-82e1-467968cd4443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(3x+2)(3x-2)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244917} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b7ee80e-437d-599e-8d92-09c467d8e2c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8007e479-04c9-545e-b360-e628f7f99f22" :question/math-academy-id "q-113540" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{3x+2}+\\frac{1}{3x-2})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{3x+2}+\\frac{1}{3x-2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(3x+2)(3x-2)|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(3x+2)(3x-2)|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac9bdca2-12f3-5135-add9-f6cee60b6017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399804} {:answer/id #uuid "ea637b56-a1b6-530a-b8ad-0581d7725118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\sqrt{3},x=0$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399805} {:answer/id #uuid "6caf38b1-8d42-5322-9446-2276f113b03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\sqrt{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399806} {:answer/id #uuid "88004c88-6f69-578a-840b-c594bbb79e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399807} {:answer/id #uuid "7aeb7239-f064-571a-82f0-341dafcdd4e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aeb7239-f064-571a-82f0-341dafcdd4e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399808} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce9f7db0-7e00-5308-89b8-2a0dcddb1466" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0085fda-c140-5022-a903-4aa2d0060987" :question/math-academy-id "q-113554" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-3)}{({x}^{2}-3)({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}}{\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068723 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fe2a279-0b92-561e-b17d-b902f43725ac" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by a Constant Multiple of Its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e32ff11-46c7-5e54-94bf-fdf37bd4e777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4+{x}^{6})^{6}}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245143} {:answer/id #uuid "61c7ef3d-13c8-5479-bf15-23c7156762ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4+{x}^{6})^{6}}{18}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245171} {:answer/id #uuid "90a0075f-a39c-584e-ab19-3bcdb240a499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4+{x}^{6})^{6}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245575} {:answer/id #uuid "ad996ae5-8424-527c-80d5-4c89b95ad520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4+{x}^{6})^{6}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245863} {:answer/id #uuid "c21643d0-8214-58cb-bb01-f4183d7d91a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(4+{x}^{6})^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61c7ef3d-13c8-5479-bf15-23c7156762ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4+{x}^{6})^{6}}{18}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245171} :answer-field/id #uuid "51b37ac4-75b0-5695-8c29-875df29f0437" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49e41322-d540-5688-b30a-e33aaf2dc9a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-113710" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 2{x}^{5}(4+{x}^{6})^{5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the term $2{x}^{5}$ outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $4+{x}^{6}.$ But the derivative is $6{x}^{5},$ so we're only out by a constant factor.\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu=4+{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{5}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{5}(4+{x}^{6})^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4+{x}^{6})^{5}\\cdot 2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{u}^{6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}(4+{x}^{6})^{6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{(4+{x}^{6})^{6}}{18}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "18ce1807-3a09-5ebd-b3cd-a91db8d2b4a8" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Using Natural Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d5dafea-bf81-547d-bc49-424b9dda654f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+2x|}{2x+2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245413} {:answer/id #uuid "99a2a79b-5577-502b-8046-72f844be5fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245676} {:answer/id #uuid "d24c27f7-0e1d-5d59-b35b-8372ae555f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+2x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246185} {:answer/id #uuid "f82296d1-fe61-58f2-9cf3-93e048824594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x}{2x+2}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246494} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9b4b11-e546-550e-884b-27bb9cfddd54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d24c27f7-0e1d-5d59-b35b-8372ae555f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+2x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246185} :answer-field/id #uuid "79f96c4c-101d-52fa-a1e7-55a417e265bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a444aaf3-15c5-5890-a26f-622f4c6bdbef" :question/math-academy-id "q-113721" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{2x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{2}+2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\cdot (2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03e934c8-da65-5615-9717-a50eee3577fd" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Other Fractional Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "387b1fd3-d0b6-5a4f-9f87-df4622619640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5(3-{x}^{5})^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244813} {:answer/id #uuid "5a108ad3-8f59-57fb-86b0-8078293f3c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{7(3-{x}^{5})^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245104} {:answer/id #uuid "64849b83-6c8e-5754-857d-65fbc670429e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{25(3-{x}^{5})^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245200} {:answer/id #uuid "92481de8-22aa-54dd-8e3e-03c362cb4b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{25(3-{x}^{5})^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245589} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b59bc3-4c29-5982-981f-e767a487ae12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7(3-{x}^{5})^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92481de8-22aa-54dd-8e3e-03c362cb4b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{25(3-{x}^{5})^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245589} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5979b9c-8e33-51cf-9331-a30916482816" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14745f6d-a16a-5fbf-98d1-0322f61ed9f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-113726" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{7{x}^{4}}{{(3-{x}^{5})}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3-{x}^{5}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{7}{5}\\text{d}u=7{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7{x}^{4}}{{(3-{x}^{5})}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{(3-{x}^{5})}^{6}}\\cdot 7{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{6}}\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}(-\\frac{1}{5{u}^{5}})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{25{u}^{5}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{25(3-{x}^{5})^{5}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054610 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "078c9361-898f-5b9f-889e-c92442f882d9" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Constant Factor Rule to Integrate a Constant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a047b88-d760-555b-9e93-164a62866d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245252} {:answer/id #uuid "95a7a62a-096c-53a6-9e63-99bffb683248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245622} {:answer/id #uuid "a51fd8e4-b513-5c94-9774-6b4bdb6b2119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245797} {:answer/id #uuid "b43d5d61-14e6-5d21-a689-c9df2d507f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245921} {:answer/id #uuid "f824863b-216c-5fb2-8443-c7b2f37cefa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f824863b-216c-5fb2-8443-c7b2f37cefa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246495} :answer-field/id #uuid "b72ce3d4-25eb-5424-b4ec-a1a9ca20b6ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e9f7c60-e13f-56df-879c-69f076975c52" :question/math-academy-id "q-113743" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f149f8ef-dd7f-5f65-928e-534e1541f9b2" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Rational Expressions with a Single Common Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3138c09d-4d43-51c5-bead-0fdb778e1372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5k}{2h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247870} {:answer/id #uuid "31b53764-ce11-5727-886a-8d35ebdf717e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{kh}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247876} {:answer/id #uuid "4536e350-3f2a-5750-abb4-9f26a11d9ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2hk}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248045} {:answer/id #uuid "a62285a7-f22e-5063-a1e7-ac6ebe678521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{k}{5h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248905} {:answer/id #uuid "eee7c8c3-97c8-59fa-9ad6-63ca4f761b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{k}{2h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eee7c8c3-97c8-59fa-9ad6-63ca4f761b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{k}{2h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249521} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a8d3447-4499-5d0e-ad45-b2bcd7eff6ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d696dd66-1554-57e6-9d74-9c8f4a560ef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-113818" :question/problem "$\\frac{5}{h}÷\\frac{10}{k}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{h}÷\\frac{10}{k},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{h}÷\\frac{10}{k} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{h}\\cdot \\frac{k}{10} & = \\\\ \\frac{5k}{10h} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}\\cdot k}{\\cancel{5}\\cdot 2h} & = \\\\ \\frac{k}{2h} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $5$ in the numerator and denominator."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7614a05-f221-5fda-b491-6893f44a1a5f" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Rational Expressions with Multiple Common Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97c1c6ab-73c5-56f5-9fee-ca0a10e54db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(z-2)}{z+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248776} {:answer/id #uuid "a9625933-0685-5510-8621-3032717dd0ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18(z-2)}{z+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248929} {:answer/id #uuid "b3efc6b8-c94f-51b9-8dc1-1921a321212b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{z+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249034} {:answer/id #uuid "badd22b5-a641-5ad8-8db4-43458737b054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18(z+1)}{z-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249093} {:answer/id #uuid "ef35f532-0726-5061-9704-c32df9534b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{z+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3efc6b8-c94f-51b9-8dc1-1921a321212b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{z+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249034} :answer-field/id #uuid "96e98c2b-f5b8-5971-a5f1-a27e14529d08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd3f875b-33f7-55de-93e3-693ddb1d2606" :question/math-academy-id "q-113826" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{3(z-2)}{(z+1)^{2}}=a$ and $\\frac{z-2}{6(z+1)}=b,$ what is $a÷b?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\na÷b=\\frac{3(z-2)}{(z+1)^{2}}÷\\frac{z-2}{6(z+1)},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(z-2)}{(z+1)^{2}}÷\\frac{z-2}{6(z+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(z-2)}{(z+1)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{6(z+1)}{z-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cancel{(z-2)}}{(z+1)^{\\cancel{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{6\\cancel{(z+1)}}{\\cancel{(z-2)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{z+1}\\cdot 6 & = \\\\ \\frac{18}{z+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096630 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3245c48f-1a4d-5b3a-9824-8d88eff85902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247883} {:answer/id #uuid "630663c5-73ef-581c-8f85-9dab63b65145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{x}^{2}+2x-2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248322} {:answer/id #uuid "78f4a7cf-7d63-5517-b755-104a70761128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{-3}^{0}(-{x}^{2}+2x-2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248484} {:answer/id #uuid "907fc2ff-2b6f-5b81-8ef6-ee353873c46f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{-3}^{0}({x}^{2}-2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248707} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f6fe33-4274-5a92-8a81-3a822cd289e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{-1}^{3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3245c48f-1a4d-5b3a-9824-8d88eff85902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247883} :answer-field/id #uuid "31720fdc-70b9-5d97-9b44-5f4fe1d54cab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63bcbbf8-c502-5d3b-bd0e-da913f8e74d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-113868" :question/problem "![](images/14/149fd953075c1d9909e036d4935ccd4f17ad1734d86bba4a70254468cd2035d5.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x+2$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x+2$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x+2$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bd8c6d8-0b11-5735-88a1-9b12e6315401" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Exponential Function With a General Base"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0558a391-cdbf-584c-a0f0-8f221f341cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{x}ln⁡5+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247467} {:answer/id #uuid "06607b0a-9d24-576c-adf1-bdc2e55f52bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247482} {:answer/id #uuid "22d652bf-3b5e-5181-8f0c-7123f5c17343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{ln⁡x}{ln⁡5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247734} {:answer/id #uuid "68d69094-f985-5c03-a478-6c0269a3cc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248365} {:answer/id #uuid "99a53d55-830f-55db-ac33-a12525e3cd5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5ln⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68d69094-f985-5c03-a478-6c0269a3cc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248365} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eca50b8-7630-5d78-accd-b706994b9510" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "333b8bc2-8d50-5f18-bbc4-8fbfe08b346d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113887" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064178 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0cae5c1b-57bd-51f6-a360-db946d25532f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sine and Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271c490c-5a05-5e0a-9c8e-cee6e93cd8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}cos⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247771} {:answer/id #uuid "39e2cf43-8568-5bca-98a7-54e900afc4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6cos⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247936} {:answer/id #uuid "8369302d-e75e-5f17-a44e-47eda2ef57fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6sin⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248585} {:answer/id #uuid "8f10e1a5-527b-5f97-860b-39eca916d8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6sin⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248696} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b91066-0304-5eec-9198-3b7d46a0b0c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6cos⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39e2cf43-8568-5bca-98a7-54e900afc4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6cos⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247936} :answer-field/id #uuid "d679155c-4e3c-59a8-a851-7bdacacd637d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4099e9a0-92e6-5376-9bf3-b36d248db1d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-113907" :question/problem "$\\int (-6sin⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int sin⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =-cos⁡\\theta +C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (-6sin⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =-6\\int sin⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-6(-cos⁡\\theta )+C \\\\  & =6cos⁡\\theta +C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064179 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e617f241-254b-5f70-9937-5883b6f28c4f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Secant-Tangent and Cosecant-Cotangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a6b2d77-6333-57e0-afd5-ae25cfed1172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3sec⁡xcot⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247800} {:answer/id #uuid "558aa8a6-6ebf-5d3f-a5a9-3b19d8e488c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3csc⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248195} {:answer/id #uuid "73fffa3b-03d4-5a40-b567-70c7ae4ccf96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3csc⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248445} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b2a2ae-e079-58a1-a369-536f12c56dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3sec⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248893} {:answer/id #uuid "acbcb4c2-9b18-5aa7-84c2-3dabda9f77dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3sec⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73fffa3b-03d4-5a40-b567-70c7ae4ccf96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3csc⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248445} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3fad684-5e9d-51a7-9e19-ea6cfd103f16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ba0c93b-e27b-584f-9b8e-5ad2b653f757" :question/math-academy-id "q-113920" :question/problem "$\\int -3csc⁡x\\,cot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-csc⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int -3csc⁡x\\,cot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3(-csc⁡x)+C \\\\  & =3csc⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf358b8-31fb-5587-8663-f414317c42be" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Secant Squared"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a394ebb-e724-5257-b2b3-c6ee24c9c0ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2sec⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247513} {:answer/id #uuid "645e7853-d773-51a7-97f0-145e363f5063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2csc⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248330} {:answer/id #uuid "a159c48f-05f8-5b71-ac43-7e38b5032b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248866} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0b4d51-0534-5643-95e4-f3c845c225d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249233} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2434c9-75b3-5a4f-85a5-13b29917e9e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2tan⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee2434c9-75b3-5a4f-85a5-13b29917e9e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2tan⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249513} :answer-field/id #uuid "0726a333-67f4-5bae-9092-988faf871214" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1eae0c2a-9fbb-58f9-9e02-1ea035b71ceb" :question/math-academy-id "q-113936" :question/problem "$\\int 2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =tan⁡\\theta +C\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =2\\int {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2tan⁡\\theta +C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b1feabb-d80c-59ec-8f66-d72d31498c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247669} {:answer/id #uuid "42c0433e-7aaf-5761-81da-33fe01ca9740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248020} {:answer/id #uuid "84f2ec16-f076-58cc-9d96-6046d0c047cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248604} {:answer/id #uuid "a2228bab-407a-5173-8ec9-6f138869205f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248871} {:answer/id #uuid "da8f8caf-52fa-57a4-9034-247fa54a5c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b1feabb-d80c-59ec-8f66-d72d31498c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247669} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bbc3278-eb17-5700-bb41-ce67c5754955" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7327f68-1eda-5ad2-867d-ca3941f336b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-114323" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(4,3)$ and the line $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](images/ea/ea64c0752738b0c917d897060351cdd43ed7bbf687cc20781b7241ee3be0ad3c.png)\n\nSince the line $x=2$ is a vertical line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(4,3)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between the $x$-coordinate of the point and $2.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|4-2|=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc625ac-31af-5204-8aa1-d135161d7f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247552} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef14e40-61a3-5e9d-8570-f753f7cc4507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247562} {:answer/id #uuid "17d12124-8c76-5d6d-96b2-2686e53d0068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247640} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6ebd32-522e-573e-b812-a47b4e052305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247836} {:answer/id #uuid "80085e2c-2862-5780-b8b7-faf2920e4807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ef14e40-61a3-5e9d-8570-f753f7cc4507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247562} :answer-field/id #uuid "54362198-2ea2-5313-8e36-1d82209afe8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "633d0fd6-e7a8-521b-a27a-54658471a6ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-114343" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,5)$ and the line $y=x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/02/021b08ba0d14e40939103d6d3e9e2004d49e5d047a5f89677b3fe542aa866f82.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=x-2$ and passes through the point $(-1,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-(x+1) \\\\ y & =-x-1+5 \\\\ y & =-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x-2 \\\\ y=-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =-x+4 \\\\ x+x & =4+2 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=1.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=1.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,5)$ and $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3+1)^{2}+(1-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,5)$ and the line $y=x-2$ is $4\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072e45bc-d35a-54a2-8ab9-5f295ebf110f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247490} {:answer/id #uuid "14581a73-ac53-505c-8447-0394a875fcd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},-\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247608} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb5a940-7e41-5de0-9f76-33adbc3d81da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248386} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6d5bb5-c38f-58f9-bdcf-296b1625b1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{11}{13},\\frac{3}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249109} {:answer/id #uuid "de549bfd-d213-57c1-ad88-fadc596d833b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "072e45bc-d35a-54a2-8ab9-5f295ebf110f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247490} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5d3e168-c279-50d0-a119-0930eb63b6db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd8e8e07-0b88-58f7-806a-13412093afb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-114345" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff9810290868f7fd465bc310c969255b92f646d6b59ad71a93a3dcc9c38ef49c.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+2x & =3 \\\\ 3y & =-2x+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1$ and passes through the point $(3,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}+5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{3}{2}x & =\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5)$ is  $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a248b79-7e8b-5ad8-b2e8-847ac2030783" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Square Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a55f584c-0261-59b4-bb28-5df04c5e5bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051333} {:answer/id #uuid "df503ec5-393a-5e2d-b982-c56e35aaa181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051334} {:answer/id #uuid "03de6fb6-f81c-5d37-a647-7b5873b78089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051335} {:answer/id #uuid "423881fd-5f5f-52b5-95c3-bdb632772bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051336} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb4b1ee-1844-5475-bfe1-7abebf91b3b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051337}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8876c980-9b7f-5aa5-b5a7-7c5722252f32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051332} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6e60215-8077-5f8c-8c55-359a0a908320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386738} {:answer/id #uuid "29d738c0-81db-5e6c-91c9-db679852e3b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386739} {:answer/id #uuid "20fa7a5b-5a7b-5d2e-91fe-afbafad3502d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386740} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d7ca1e-94f6-54ec-80f6-9ab401480cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386741} {:answer/id #uuid "8751a674-6cb2-575b-98a1-e1863e33ff8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1d7ca1e-94f6-54ec-80f6-9ab401480cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386741} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ce63358-0fcc-55be-a22f-c3c4afd97cf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06528850-f3e1-5360-9392-bba2a3bf7deb" :question/math-academy-id "q-11440" :question/problem "Calculate $A+B$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+4 & 5+1 \\\\ 3+(-2) & 1+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f247351-8c81-595e-bb30-26630159819c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Missing Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "486cff62-01d3-5049-9232-7e6b8711ae02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248073} {:answer/id #uuid "735df79d-ddb4-5640-bc31-d30cdbd2b16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248433} {:answer/id #uuid "a517b323-5082-5ab0-976c-3adc8f5377c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248898} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0187fb-9d9a-5be9-8904-397388709c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249085} {:answer/id #uuid "beede94a-cbfc-5628-8c7c-ee54767e0e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "735df79d-ddb4-5640-bc31-d30cdbd2b16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248433} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d4f1ea4-5984-59d1-8aad-4caa825a8331" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76685609-49ea-56b3-87fb-774aa7271a5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115101" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{5x-22}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{5}{x-4}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-4)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-22 & =5(x-4)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=4$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(4)-22 & =5(4-4)+B \\\\ -2 & =0+B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de557d1d-5f27-50e3-bcba-22b21f83267f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "153668da-d780-523a-8e26-48e058715b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247615} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9dc02d-1da3-5526-af85-e13cc50a7c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247968} {:answer/id #uuid "750c26fb-43a5-5a41-8361-58c46e0b0804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248452} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d902f3-35d6-5d0d-ac8b-52610f9c819b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248870} {:answer/id #uuid "a8569992-7633-5a8b-9630-e609ef25ddfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "750c26fb-43a5-5a41-8361-58c46e0b0804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248452} :answer-field/id #uuid "106279dc-5bad-5b89-83c9-6a40e4f9b6f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "522f5175-a019-5a90-95ed-58cc045a0980" :question/math-academy-id "q-115124" :question/problem "![](images/76/764f25a4b18f3f28110c0ce3345402bf183d61cac350832663e221b0de9852a5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e43e393-8752-58bc-b87c-b72f679cbfd9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4ffa0ff-df0b-5bc5-b896-3f6772a35019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$28$ and $24$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399852} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f1177f-3e11-593f-ad9c-dda3299fba50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$24.5$ and $18$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399853} {:answer/id #uuid "e59ee665-26a4-5d2e-8c55-26143f2480d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399854} {:answer/id #uuid "ee829b75-97f1-545e-8cf4-3704340f4ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$49$ and $36$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399855} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e390e1-9f85-5321-a954-3f147b97e82b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e59ee665-26a4-5d2e-8c55-26143f2480d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399854} :answer-field/id #uuid "44102d96-20a4-51fc-add2-7a7a026df6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3113828e-6ca6-530b-804d-2cdc91aade73" :question/math-academy-id "q-115134" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=49$ and ${b}^{2}=36.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=49,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=36,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=6.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 6=12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e9c4ef2-0491-5b42-8679-4fe7dfbd7b00" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Partial Fraction Decomposition With Higher Order Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dbca64e-b64c-56ce-8eb6-ecc6fa7d24a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247969} {:answer/id #uuid "7dcc2019-801d-5716-bfaf-80f9a09a03a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248526} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2be079-dea0-55fc-b357-78c9dfb9a15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248655} {:answer/id #uuid "8eeafaf3-5a99-5006-982b-a9ce701b9d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248693} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ebd06e-cdc5-52a8-b4dd-4e79454aff1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a2be079-dea0-55fc-b357-78c9dfb9a15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248655} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f468e2b-e0a3-5878-a496-8f74d00ba6ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a9eca33-ce1c-5c81-8c1b-83778e94422b" :question/math-academy-id "q-115214" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{(x-2)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$, $B$ and $C$ are real constants, find the value of $A\\cdot B\\cdot C.$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-2)^{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+9 & =A(x-2)^{2}+B(x-2)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=2$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2}-6(2)+9 & =A(2-2)^{2}+B(2-2)+C \\\\ 1 & =A(0)+B(0)+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-6(0)+9 & =A(0-2)^{2}+B(0-2)+1 \\\\ 9 & =4A-2B+1 \\\\ 0 & =4A-2B-8 \\\\ 0 & =2A-B-4 \\\\ B & =2A-4.\\,\\,(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, letting $x=1$ and using our value for $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2}-6(1)+9 & =A(1-2)^{2}+B(1-2)+1 \\\\ 4 & =A-B+1 \\\\ 0 & =A-B-3 \\\\ B & =A-3.\\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom $(1)$ and $(2)$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A-4 & =A-3 \\\\ 2A-A & =-3+4 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the value of $A$ into $(2)$  to get the value of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =A-3=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B\\cdot C & =1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53855cff-bcca-509b-8df4-37d79e809e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399873} {:answer/id #uuid "17302a0c-db71-5e47-9f9c-7d55635451b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399874} {:answer/id #uuid "a6aed6ae-4085-506e-b702-8b9d95570e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $2\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399875} {:answer/id #uuid "f17c689d-e9fa-5fd9-b2ff-5ea750ce8ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399876} {:answer/id #uuid "d786fe68-0f02-5397-bafb-ab8b7df3332f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12.5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6aed6ae-4085-506e-b702-8b9d95570e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $2\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399875} :answer-field/id #uuid "632f4b79-0b5d-599f-9a9d-155f44ffca58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6821b43a-88ac-5b4b-9151-2f35f973a4c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115313" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{6}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-5$ and $k=2,$ so the center is located at $(-5,2).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=25$ and ${b}^{2}=6.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=25,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=6,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 5=10,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{6}=2\\sqrt{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100994 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e9df1ae-8176-555b-987d-94773c27d435" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of a Circle on the Coordinate Plane Using the Distance Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2172b8f7-3593-5571-b2f3-7d7267020abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247720} {:answer/id #uuid "39cf299f-3302-568e-80c6-f68f90479350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247934} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb4096d-f189-5fca-82c4-8803e1e12ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248291} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0f207e-3753-5536-ba31-32a146709a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248381} {:answer/id #uuid "dca5ff4b-c86a-5b8a-b547-f8671207d2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c0f207e-3753-5536-ba31-32a146709a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248381} :answer-field/id #uuid "cffa1467-5032-5c7c-8355-c1c0efa2e0bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6fa395af-9563-5dd6-b788-298fc3edabb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-115360" :question/problem "The points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(-3,-4)$ and $(0,-4)$, respectively, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle. Find the radius of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The diameter can be calculated using the distance formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3-0)^{2}+(-4-(-4))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is half the diameter:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{d}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100995 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52384502-66c5-5089-b38f-d46b94877430" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Radius of a Circle on the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8fa6bba-8366-50e3-bd6e-0487665ca2c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,3)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399887} {:answer/id #uuid "0fba675a-9578-5d7e-86a6-668cee3df4ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,4)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399888} {:answer/id #uuid "73d0aee8-773e-58fb-89a9-36098d20a922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,4)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399889} {:answer/id #uuid "d14d98e0-0f4f-5aa7-b716-c80926e97a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,3)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399890} {:answer/id #uuid "4de2cf95-13f1-5b1b-8916-4cd2aebe378f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,3)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4de2cf95-13f1-5b1b-8916-4cd2aebe378f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,3)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399891} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a898ac5-9ac8-57ea-9a3a-e0a38e6b53ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a02e3599-0d24-591c-985f-c45e9f652ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-115364" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3af3101fd7a103e8382755177ca1ebab9bc1cdf48aebd34af8ac7cc92991387c.png)\n\nGiven that the points $(-1,2)$ and $(1,4)$ lie on a diameter of a circle, what are the center and radius of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. So, we can calculate the coordinates of the center using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(h,k) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-1+1}{2},\\frac{2+4}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{0}{2},\\frac{6}{2}) \\\\  & =(0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius can be found by calculating the distance between the center $(0,3)$ and a point on the circle, such as $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1-0)}^{2}+{(2-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096693 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "281514e9-6e16-5b21-ba4e-503991d811c2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80063ee4-5a2e-54d5-a7c0-7f53301ee070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248547} {:answer/id #uuid "942c6f19-e871-5efb-bc84-cc274cf8d7e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248741} {:answer/id #uuid "aa477baf-669c-5ca1-9535-44663ae87ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=16y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248939} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d8ef81-1c40-56e6-b62a-55e8bb45c5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249148} {:answer/id #uuid "f0990477-9c09-5cd5-b650-146fa848ec1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1d8ef81-1c40-56e6-b62a-55e8bb45c5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249148} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fc0ca73-8e47-548c-a716-86b46758af6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88252d6a-eb99-5de5-8ab4-de1f72848e55" :question/math-academy-id "q-115432" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(2,1),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4p(1) \\\\ 4 & =4p \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(1)y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5ff86e-cbd2-5244-bd7e-fa7501ce1356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=2(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247533} {:answer/id #uuid "6d01c7a7-ed12-581a-857f-30f616b9ef99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248388} {:answer/id #uuid "711453e3-73f9-5722-820f-ac643ba5fcb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248413} {:answer/id #uuid "8d999a07-30ce-573b-8c2d-fa8220991104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=4(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248686} {:answer/id #uuid "d6aed572-9d95-5182-8bd1-9ea1faa87eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c5ff86e-cbd2-5244-bd7e-fa7501ce1356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=2(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247533} :answer-field/id #uuid "242c488e-b1d2-5f9b-924c-0c83035c9cef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af2d74a7-36e3-5f6f-95f7-10b5d7e5decf" :question/math-academy-id "q-115451" :question/problem "![](images/97/970db64a0ddd325993323511ad4ae754ce64f64d591877bfa0ac908874e2393f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,1).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y-1).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,3),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=3$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (4-2)^{2} & =4p(3-1) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =2(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "448f46ef-d3ea-5129-b534-69738ce2bf22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=12(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248036} {:answer/id #uuid "61d14d47-1b22-5269-bf65-c45079b61a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-12(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248307} {:answer/id #uuid "96e2c46f-fcee-5729-87c3-3a89d53c7346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-3(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248769} {:answer/id #uuid "c2981009-de70-58f9-bf1b-475e3952da5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{5}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249154} {:answer/id #uuid "d1808623-7ad0-5bda-a910-99c16db5520e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{5}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61d14d47-1b22-5269-bf65-c45079b61a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-12(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248307} :answer-field/id #uuid "7857cb4e-c7be-5b90-8d5b-a7a24b7b056d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3ec34c5-8643-5bc8-b96a-2dfe405443a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-115502" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c6b336b55e48a68dcad83efc4a8acd7659225b8f8e29faf8ea176c2cead29901.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,1).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y-1).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-4,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (-4-2)^{2} & =4p(-2-1) \\\\ 36 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(-3)(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-12(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a986a9-dc99-5796-9957-e44b0b8d9b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247416} {:answer/id #uuid "3af46962-42bf-52d0-9044-0ca1c67c1f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247946} {:answer/id #uuid "40f40afb-bd2f-53ad-bfee-4cedf94cb572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-4(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248000} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8a6a65-91c8-5dc8-8832-b63911bb283a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249388} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0b7d8e-f5b6-5077-a852-7535675aef4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{16}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd8a6a65-91c8-5dc8-8832-b63911bb283a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249388} :answer-field/id #uuid "a77272b0-ce4e-560d-9231-c7789014a60c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55c7e4a7-0bdd-5125-954e-1f3a2bdbf235" :question/math-academy-id "q-115513" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(4,0)$ and which passes through the point $(1,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(4,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,-4),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (1-4)^{2} & =4p(-4) \\\\ 9 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{9}{16})y \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =-\\frac{9}{4}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17099881-d7b9-54d9-a29a-4f5f28561cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45166553-bf16-5fa9-8f50-1ec180cc0491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{16}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248043} {:answer/id #uuid "869aa896-1386-5aa1-acd0-77b1926c3156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248625} {:answer/id #uuid "b1716195-2d70-5b16-8e29-18c8d7c74eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-16x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249013} {:answer/id #uuid "cad70ee1-b661-5e1b-8e0a-c2d157b64a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249231} {:answer/id #uuid "d07d22e0-e6d0-514f-92c9-e09c9996024c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1716195-2d70-5b16-8e29-18c8d7c74eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-16x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249013} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2c6191f-54f1-5592-9add-c8743f8cc4c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07da239d-b796-56b0-87bd-44779be7e860" :question/math-academy-id "q-115634" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-1,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p(-1) \\\\ 16 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-4)x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-16x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096704 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2674032e-cd4e-5e8a-803c-317914ccae04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247762} {:answer/id #uuid "839ff22d-c37d-5d91-9333-174668c3261a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{4}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248587} {:answer/id #uuid "ba27305a-d65b-5604-aaff-169ce339bb7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249088} {:answer/id #uuid "c6443236-97c8-5e59-91fe-9a22a7cec2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=9(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249186} {:answer/id #uuid "dc878183-0dd0-5e61-ae12-26bab8ba8369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{12}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2674032e-cd4e-5e8a-803c-317914ccae04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247762} :answer-field/id #uuid "53f52642-018d-53e4-af4e-c4f058d871f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fff1310e-72c2-5e26-9574-a4434ee14fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-115642" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8ea1ad85f30978bed681f48e02f4af2f401a1f561390bee09683ead252fc1d7d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(1,-2)$ and passes through the point $(4,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,-2),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=-2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,-3),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-3$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (-3+2)^{2} & =4p(4-1) \\\\ 1 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{12})(x-1) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{12}$ is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096705 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "783ca59b-0242-584c-ae47-004a0eb03817" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given the Vertex and Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1250a4f0-3718-52c9-9904-980960e90f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=-(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247590} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef0ec03-c73b-5bdb-afad-8cb320620573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247845} {:answer/id #uuid "9501f1cc-1079-55a6-ae61-087d9b006e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=4(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248747} {:answer/id #uuid "e72f2ce1-958b-58a2-8358-7d53986a1a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=4(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249460} {:answer/id #uuid "f753f4ac-c307-5fb5-a501-f7cc4a07aeb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-4(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e72f2ce1-958b-58a2-8358-7d53986a1a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=4(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249460} :answer-field/id #uuid "07d422bf-e80a-5532-a92f-ce3f3b453131" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4025b5ab-d584-549e-8e48-80f0cd57c2e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-115644" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-1)$ and passes through the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,-1),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=-1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(2,1),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (1+1)^{2} & =4p(2-1) \\\\ 4 & =4p \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(1)(x-1) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "beab791b-0819-5768-9e5f-0dc621d5a462" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Two Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bbe6c94-b151-5c1d-9c24-6ac6eaedff7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247527} {:answer/id #uuid "284a2d2d-aec2-56b2-a83a-447fe88b345e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247782} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd2d177-bb80-5fbb-b4fb-239d54aa4023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248101} {:answer/id #uuid "9a88c5c8-97bc-5272-bbaa-7a0f74513dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248802} {:answer/id #uuid "bfeb1cf3-0a3b-5e0b-81f5-f88d8973a54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bbe6c94-b151-5c1d-9c24-6ac6eaedff7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247527} :answer-field/id #uuid "929d68dd-7d98-50ea-b0e6-bb084af9ebee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a05ffdc-3b52-5cee-9869-369fd44c61d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-115794" :question/problem "Which value of $z$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $z=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3z$ and $6z.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3z,6z)=6z.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6z,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z} \\\\ 6z\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{6z}) & =6z\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}) \\\\ 6z\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{6z}) & =6z\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{3z})+6z\\cdot (\\frac{z-1}{6z}) \\\\ \\cancel{6z}\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{\\cancel{6z}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{3z}\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{\\cancel{3z}})+\\cancel{6z}\\cdot (\\frac{z-1}{\\cancel{6z}}) \\\\ 2z-1 & =2\\cdot (3z-1)+z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $z,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-1 & =2(3z-1)+z-1 \\\\ 2z-1 & =6z-2+z-1 \\\\ 2z-1 & =7z-3 \\\\ 2z-7z & =-3+1 \\\\ -5z & =-2 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $z=0$ as a solution. But since $z=\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $z=\\frac{2}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d27b85e4-ddae-5b5f-965a-5dff51b8449b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vertex of a Vertical Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ff28b3-a4ef-5634-a723-9c7e9fe2ab39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247714} {:answer/id #uuid "98d4e748-2168-5cb4-bb3d-c851125d1b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248788} {:answer/id #uuid "abcbd97a-5003-5b7a-8d19-a85482f993a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248952} {:answer/id #uuid "cecdc329-36c7-5062-90f6-c9e193b82b66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249256} {:answer/id #uuid "e601ffa8-cd81-5d33-b1c2-e93f104c920b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cecdc329-36c7-5062-90f6-c9e193b82b66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249256} :answer-field/id #uuid "858767bb-4413-5728-bc93-27047974679c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47ce069d-e0d7-5b9d-a02a-3975e0c397e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-115881" :question/problem "![](images/72/725cbaa4ce797e0436d1a53d4b1a9eedad5a15282fadfa8cb8f3381135e92d87.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of an upward-opening parabola is the lowest point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/80/80f4e7505c204572ecbc3c2e1c207fa9f321bc9925f5e2e947db7feee45824f9.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(6,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e302810-8b14-5c8c-8bbb-245386a66b7c" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Vertex of a Horizontal Parabola Given Its Equation in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "106bf08a-6288-5161-b589-b3a6bfd5d596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247568} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecf6f9d-a2f5-586a-b320-3685a62fe9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248538} {:answer/id #uuid "9d436ef0-528d-5c42-acfd-fecb45ade3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248822} {:answer/id #uuid "e75adbb9-dff5-55d0-b063-3054aec88c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249463} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0d9b69-2f3f-545f-8357-505ac2a681cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d436ef0-528d-5c42-acfd-fecb45ade3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248822} :answer-field/id #uuid "791f4c31-853e-56a1-9a6c-8d9759f6db39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ec04397-a1d2-5a43-be90-9c3301fd7dc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115911" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(y-1)^{2}=5(x+7)?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}=4(\\frac{5}{4})(x+7).\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-7$ and $k=1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-7,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdab0134-e416-51cf-89fa-73ad4a2c2eb9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "316d7132-e4d2-552b-8229-76cf4a760b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247871} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9febdf-840d-5e4a-9454-270334fac866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248674} {:answer/id #uuid "bb19e8fc-2e30-562b-bb53-409259921842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249096} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb9b84b-92e1-5992-bbf6-9dbd59a7b2b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249366} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d8c934-c5fd-5811-a685-9aa2936725ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3d8c934-c5fd-5811-a685-9aa2936725ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249432} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb9b725b-1554-5fc4-b7bb-be67fcd944ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35bb8fc9-b273-5104-bbf5-373700e22296" :question/math-academy-id "q-116100" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=-1$ and $k=5.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-1,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdab0134-e416-51cf-89fa-73ad4a2c2eb9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d15b2a-d840-5012-b070-e3462c9da9d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(36,16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247594} {:answer/id #uuid "247c9c2b-5862-5eab-b7d6-5bc4e81f7699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247743} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c70e60-f026-55b3-9d72-4a1fbcbe300c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(16,36)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248861} {:answer/id #uuid "dd02f9e6-3fef-5510-9fef-c8504811fe08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249383} {:answer/id #uuid "e539cb36-7d2c-587b-a892-73405cb109ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e539cb36-7d2c-587b-a892-73405cb109ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249442} :answer-field/id #uuid "667ba501-b342-5000-bcdf-f4159e0d68ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2a54ae2-f488-56eb-9b24-9f9d28627e54" :question/math-academy-id "q-116103" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{36}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-(-4))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-(-6))^{2}}{{6}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=-6$ and $k=-4.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-6,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c698912-1f35-5110-8d5d-d490cce2e9a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Quadratic Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0237b559-7d33-5cd0-9da8-8d8b205f4742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247438} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb99e31-a544-5220-80a8-c1892b899c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247705} {:answer/id #uuid "cac77d12-ae34-5ddd-8afb-806b1d42068b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249230} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d99394-c6b7-5118-9b32-40e3e577b39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249294} {:answer/id #uuid "fc368912-431d-5c1c-94c8-d1e252061d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2d99394-c6b7-5118-9b32-40e3e577b39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249294} :answer-field/id #uuid "05de44ca-2588-5951-97b7-06f8b0b05597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f403d57-f6d2-5901-9ad6-4def1edb7127" :question/math-academy-id "q-116104" :question/problem "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $x$ and ${x}^{2}.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,{x}^{2})={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${x}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x})-{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}) & =x\\cdot \\cancel{x}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{\\cancel{x}})-\\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{x-3}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}) \\\\ (3x-1) & =x\\cdot 2-(x-3) \\\\ (3x-1) & =2x-(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-1) & =2x-(x-3) \\\\ 3x-1 & =2x-x+3 \\\\ 3x-1 & =x+3 \\\\ 3x-x & =3+1 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083206 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "345d6df7-2ada-5c2e-8146-7c779afae3af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da8f012-4b8e-59d2-86f2-6cf9ded76584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247549} {:answer/id #uuid "1d73eb71-f3b0-5802-bd97-3518478f6143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247688} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6e6b00-01a7-50ba-8b49-1508e2c1ebb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248267} {:answer/id #uuid "93a15523-e8ae-509a-8f92-1e5d130afcfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248737} {:answer/id #uuid "f70239d3-779d-5762-a4c5-9498427f2ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c6e6b00-01a7-50ba-8b49-1508e2c1ebb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248267} :answer-field/id #uuid "465002b5-f411-5a4c-a8ea-09bf4dac334e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "09ea155c-8ce4-5cf1-a51e-cc7b1072dc21" :question/math-academy-id "q-116116" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $6{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}-60y+8x=-122?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}-60y+8x & =-122 \\\\ 6{y}^{2}-60y-4{x}^{2}+8x & =-122 \\\\ 6[{y}^{2}-10y]-4[{x}^{2}-2x] & =-122 \\\\ 6[(y-5)^{2}-25]-4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =-122 \\\\ 6(y-5)^{2}-150-4(x-1)^{2}+4 & =-122 \\\\ 6(y-5)^{2}-4(x-1)^{2} & =-122+150-4 \\\\ 6(y-5)^{2}-4(x-1)^{2} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(1,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087860 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e75012ec-8203-574b-ba07-0a26e4cf4691" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given Endpoints of a Diameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58b042e9-ade1-5935-8496-7739089d2bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251280} {:answer/id #uuid "7dabc331-8583-5eea-8639-2e4e3b6c2e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251605} {:answer/id #uuid "b16389d5-034e-5416-a93f-c8ca3c3cb569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252105} {:answer/id #uuid "cd64e8ae-6767-597d-94ad-605f327334be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252363} {:answer/id #uuid "f51bee25-4a1e-5318-a9c6-0478a7be24bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b16389d5-034e-5416-a93f-c8ca3c3cb569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252105} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d216b22-c302-5a1c-933a-a0dd19c71347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e38a793-c9a3-5cf1-9d57-1f88f87f48f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-116927" :question/problem "![](images/34/343897550f0f7b9e4a479e47c9191ababa31070dac79dbf4d54befe869661a48.png)\n\nGiven that the points $X$ and $Y$ with coordinates $(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2),$ respectively, are the endpoints of a diameter of a circle centered at the origin, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a circle with radius $r,$ centered at the origin, is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the length of the diameter $\\overset{―}{XY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(-1-1)^{2}+(2-(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter, the radius is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110568 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69d6ed5b-1c29-53a7-a37e-817042a02861" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e370276-dd3a-5964-9ce3-7cf246014e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/62/628d53eca69ad50cab7557ede3a7f418264203fefd54adac965a7f7f446ece6c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386843} {:answer/id #uuid "ffedee4a-7324-59fb-a847-2555b4c482e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386844} {:answer/id #uuid "52d82c0b-587c-5e13-b7a8-b445f168e155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d1cb152b659ff0b75f70c8c7235357790d81c673df270de6b8dcb44bced33f39.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386845} {:answer/id #uuid "69cca405-61ef-5beb-98b6-f0ac8830d33e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d7/d75e53dde04a14f588e55f409f48c7dfbaca38c76f27a0deabe7b16b97c2a789.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386846} {:answer/id #uuid "071a1b5d-cfe5-55c4-abfe-64aaa7d45f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :